:: The Late Night [event main_quest] {"position":"3950,500","size":"100,100"}
/* Quest "The Late Night" (main_lateNight), STAGE1_PROPOSAL.md 4.3. The Veil project is done (its onComplete made the device
   owned); tonight $ethan.name and I set its field strength. Before he comes back with the pizza I switch the vent unit to Off
   (STAGE1_PROPOSAL.md 3.3): I want to know if he still laughs at my jokes without it. He does. Nobody mentions it, and he can
   never know, because the logs say the vent was on for the weeks before and after.
   The warm scene: pizza, the old headset, the startup that cut him out ("you'll get your piece", the equity seed for The
   Profit Party). At the end, alone, I switch the Veil on in the empty office and feel it settle over me. Silence.
   The vent goes back to the level it had (_lnVent) when he has left. */
<<silent>>
	<<SetBackground $locations[13].img>>
	<<set _lnVent to $company.vent.level>>
<</silent>>
$ethan.name went to get the pizza, which means I have about twenty minutes, because the place on the corner is slow and he'll stand at the counter and make friends with whoever is working.

The office is empty. Half the lights are off. In the middle of the main room, on the cart $brian.name borrowed from the building's janitor and never gave back, sits the $company.devices.valenxVeil.name: a matte gray disk about the size of a dinner plate, ringed with eight small emitters, cables running from it to two laptops on the nearest desk. It looks like a smoke detector that went to college.

Above it, in the ceiling, the vent hums the way it has hummed every working day since we moved in.

<<linkreplace "Turn the vent off">>
I open the device strip on my phone and slide the vent unit down to Off.

The hum doesn't change. It never did, the hum is just the air. But something in the room changes anyway, or I imagine it does: the light seems a little flatter, the empty desks a little more like empty desks. I've had it running at eighteen percent for so long that I'd forgotten what this office feels like on its own.

I want to know something tonight, and I want to know it for real. $ethan.name has laughed at every joke I've made since the day he walked in here. I'd like to find out how many of them were funny.

<<silently>><<run setup.setVentLevel(0)>><</silently>>

<<linkreplace "Wait for the pizza">>
He kicks the door open with one foot, two boxes balanced on one hand and a bag of garlic knots in his teeth. He drops all of it on the desk next to the laptops and holds up both hands like a magician.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $ethan, text: "One pepperoni, one with the weird stuff. Artichoke, I think. Maybe a mushroom. The guy said it was his favorite and I didn't have the heart to say no." },
{ who: $player, text: "So you bought a pizza because the pizza guy was sad." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "He wasn't sad, he was passionate. There's a difference. Also, he gave me the knots for free." },
{ text: "He grabs a slice of the weird one, folds it in half and eats a third of it in one bite while sitting down in front of the left laptop." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Okay. Field strength. Let's make your baby sing, boss." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Get to work">>
It takes three hours.

The idea is simple: find the setting where the whole room gets the effect evenly, without a hot spot under the disk and without anything leaking out into the hallway. The work is not simple. $ethan.name sends the emitters a pattern, I walk the room with the meter $brian.name built, calling out numbers from every corner, and $ethan.name adjusts and swears and sends it again. Forty times. Fifty.

Somewhere around the twentieth round I make a joke about the meter, about how $brian.name probably named it, and $ethan.name laughs so hard he has to put his slice down.

He laughs at the next one too. Not every one. He groans at a pun and throws a garlic knot at my head for another. But the good ones land, and the bad ones get exactly the eye roll they deserve, and the vent overhead is just a vent.

I don't say anything about it. Neither does he. He doesn't know there's anything to say.

<<linkreplace "Take a break">>
Around ten we stop and sit on two desks with the second pizza between us. The disk is humming on its cart at the setting we've got so far, which is close. $ethan.name has his feet on a chair and a can of soda balanced on his stomach.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $ethan, text: "You know what this reminds me of? The physics lab, the night before the project fair. You, me, and that bike helmet with the baby monitor taped to it." },
{ who: $player, text: "Oh no. Here we go." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Oh, I'm going to. The janitor came by every hour to check if we were still alive, and at two in the morning you finally looked at my code and said, and I quote, 'Okay, this doesn't completely suck'." },
{ who: $player, text: "That was a compliment. You know that was a compliment." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "From you? That was basically a medal. And the thing worked, man. The next day I sat in that gym with the helmet on for three hours, listening to a few hundred people breathing, and I thought, okay, this guy is going somewhere, and I want to be in the car when he does." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "And then we graduated and I didn't hear from you for a whole year. Which, for the record, was rude." },
{ who: $player, text: "I was busy going broke. It's a full-time job." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

He grins and tips his can at the disk.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $ethan, text: "And now look. We built a thing that doesn't just listen. It talks back. Softly." },
{ who: $player, text: "Softly." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "That's the part I like. Seriously. You could have made this thing a hammer. You made it a blanket." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I take another slice so I don't have to answer that.

<<linkreplace "Ask about his old job">>
<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "What happened at your last place? You never really said." },
{ text: "He's quiet for a moment. Then he puts the can down on the desk, very carefully, like it might spill something other than soda." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Ah. The startup. So, right after graduation, three guys from the business school had an app idea and no clue how to build it, and they found me. I wrote most of it. Like, the core of it, the part that actually worked. They did the slides. They were very good at slides." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "There was supposed to be equity. Everybody said so, at every dinner, every late night. 'When we close the round, we'll do the paperwork.' And then they closed the round, and there was a lawyer, and there was paperwork, and my name wasn't on it." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "They offered me a raise. A nice one. And I said thanks, and I cleaned out my desk, and I told myself, never again. Never again do I build somebody else's thing for a handshake." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

He says it lightly, the way he says everything. His hands don't say it lightly. One of them is turning the can in slow circles on the desk.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $ethan, text: "Anyway. Then my old lab partner called me out of nowhere and asked if I wanted to build a thing that talks back. Softly. And I said yes before he got to the salary." },
{ who: $player, text: "You'll get your piece. When this is real, you get your piece. I mean it." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "I know, boss." },
{ text: "He picks the can back up and clinks it against my slice of pizza." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "That's why I'm here." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Finish the job">>
We get it right at twenty past eleven. Every corner of the room reads the same on $brian.name's meter, give or take a decimal, and nothing at all comes through the wall into the hallway. $ethan.name saves the profile three times, under three names, the last one being <i>FINAL_final_actually_final</i>, and then he stands up and stretches until his back cracks.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $ethan, text: "That's a wrap. I'm going home to sleep for a hundred years. You want me to leave the knots?" },
{ who: $player, text: "Leave the knots." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Good man. Don't stay too late. And don't switch it on and sit in it all night, I know you." },
{ text: "He points at me from the door like a disappointed coach, and then he's gone, and I hear him whistling in the stairwell all the way down." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Switch it on">>
I turn off the rest of the lights first. Then I sit down in the middle of the empty office, in $darius.name's chair, which is the most comfortable one, with the laptop on my knees, and load the final profile.

<<DevicePulse 2>>

There's no sound. The disk doesn't glow or click. The eight little emitters just start, all at once, and the air in the room changes the way air changes before rain.

It comes over me slowly, like a warm hand on the back of the neck. My shoulders drop. The thoughts I had lined up for tomorrow, the emails, the invoices, $darius.name's notebook, all go quiet, one after the other, like someone walking through a house turning off lamps. I feel good. Calm and good and willing, and it would be so easy to sit here and agree with anything.

That's at a gentle setting, in an empty room, on me, and I know exactly what it is.

I picture the lecture hall. Two hundred students, all of them sitting under this. I picture a conference room full of people trying to make a decision. I picture a whole floor of an office tower.

I sit in the dark for a long time and let it work on me. At some point I notice I'm smiling, and that it isn't the device doing it.

<<silently>><<run setup.setVentLevel(_lnVent)>><</silently>>
Before I leave, I switch the vent back on.

<<UpdateStat "+" "Corruption" 2>>
<<UpdateStat "+" "Morale" 2>>
